<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=plate-tectonics">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plate tectonics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plate tectonics is the scientific theory that Earth's lithosphere comprises a number of large plates that have been slowly moving since 3–4 billion years ago. It builds on the continental drift described by the meteorologist Alfred Wegener in 1912 and came to be accepted after seafloor spreading was validated in the mid- to late 1960s. The theory was defined in a series of papers between 1965 and 1967, and a symposium at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society of London</a> in 1965 is regarded as the official start of its acceptance. Relative plate movement typically ranges from zero to 10 cm annually; plate boundaries are sites of earthquakes and volcanic activity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Long rejected, the idea was vindicated by <b>plate tectonics</b> in the 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-6">6 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1965–1967</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Royal Society of London</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Alfred Wegener · Tuzo Wilson · W. Jason Morgan · Harry Hess</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Alfred Wegener described continental drift in 1912, which culminated fifty years later in the theory. It was not widely accepted at first, mainly for lack of a physically supported mechanism.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=plate-tectonics">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
